1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,323
Book Three, Proposition Three.

2
00:00:02,323 --> 00:00:13,171
If in a circle a segment through the center bisect a segment not through the center, it also cuts it at right angles; and if it cut it at right angles, it also bisects it.

3
00:00:13,171 --> 00:00:20,530
Let A B C be a circle, and in it let a diameter C D through the center meet a chord A B not through the center at F.

4
00:00:20,950 --> 00:00:28,000
Take the center E of the circle A B C, and join the radii E A and E B.

5
00:00:28,420 --> 00:00:30,800
First, let C D bisect A B at F.

6
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:41,012
Since A F equals F B, F E is common, and the radii E A and E B are equal, by One Eight the adjacent angles A F E and B F E are equal.

7
00:00:41,012 --> 00:00:47,461
Therefore each is a right angle by Definition Ten, and C D cuts A B at right angles.

8
00:00:47,461 --> 00:00:51,223
Conversely, let C D cut A B at right angles at F.

9
00:00:51,223 --> 00:00:58,364
Since the radii E A and E B are equal, by One Five the base angles E A F and E B F are equal.

10
00:00:58,364 --> 00:01:12,338
In triangles E A F and E B F, two angles equal two angles and the common side E F subtends equal angles; therefore by One Twenty-Six the remaining sides are equal, so A F equals F B.

11
00:01:12,338 --> 00:01:19,094
Thus a line through the center bisects a chord if and only if it is perpendicular to it.

12
00:01:19,094 --> 00:01:20,860
Which was to be proved.

13
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:26,983
As Heath observes, Proposition Three Three establishes the two partial converses of the Porism to Three One.

14
00:01:26,983 --> 00:01:38,125
Together they form a complete triad of circle symmetry: for any line meeting a chord, any two of the three conditions—passing through the center, bisecting the chord, and cutting at right angles—imply the third.

15
00:01:38,125 --> 00:01:53,280
